#sol-item(1, "C")[
  $P("not" A) = 1 - P(A) = 1 - frac(9, 10) = frac(1, 10)$. *Chapter 10.*
]

#sol-item(2, "B")[
  $P("at least one") = 1 - (1 - frac(2, 7))^3 = frac(218, 343)$. Multiply the failures, then subtract from $1$. *Chapter 10.*
]

#sol-item(3, "A")[
  After one red has gone there are $6$ reds left in $8$ balls, so the probability is $7/9 times 6/8 = frac(7, 12)$. *Chapter 10.*
]

#sol-item(4, "D")[
  $P("at least one head") = 1 - P("no head") = 1 - 1/8 = frac(7, 8)$. *Chapter 10.*
]

#sol-item(5, "B")[
  Two orders count: red then blue, and blue then red. Their probabilities are $6/11 times 5/10$ and $5/11 times 6/10$, and they add to $frac(6, 11)$. *Chapter 10.*
]

#sol-item(6, "C")[
  $P("at least one") = 1 - (1 - frac(1, 4))^3 = frac(37, 64)$. Multiply the failures, then subtract from $1$. *Chapter 10.*
]

#sol-item(7, "D")[
  $P(A "or" B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A "and" B) = frac(1, 2) + frac(1, 3) - frac(1, 6) = frac(2, 3)$. The overlap is counted twice unless it is subtracted once. *Chapter 10.*
]

#sol-item(8, "A")[
